This solution meets the goal. The Templates blade in the Azure portal enables you to store JavaScript
Object Notation (JSON) documents that automate Azure resource deployment. In this case, to
automate the deployment of 20 additional Linux VMs based on VM1's configuration, you can use an
Azure Resource Manager (ARM) template. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Export VM1's Configuration: You should go to the Azure portal, navigate to RG1 and select VM1. Click
on "Export template" to get the ARM template for VM1.
2. Modify the ARM Template: You should download the exported template. Open the template file in a
text editor or an Integrated Development Environment (IDE). Identify the parameters section and
add parameters for the new VMs (e.g., VM names, IP addresses).
3. Create a Parameter File: Create a new JSON file for parameters. Define the parameters for each of
the 20 new VMs. This includes VM names, sizes, and other configurations.
4. Deploy the Template: Use Azure CLI or Azure PowerShell to deploy the template with the parameter
file. Here's an example of how you can deploy the template using Azure CLI: az deployment group
create -- resource-group RG1 -- template-file path/to/your/template. json --
parameters @path/to/your/parameters. json
5. Automate the Deployment: You can automate this process further by creating a script that updates
the parameter file and triggers the deployment. Here is a simple example using a shell script:
# ! /bin/bash

RESOURCE GROUP="RG1"

TEMPLATE FILE="path/to/your/template. json"
PARAMETERS FILE="path/to/your/parameters. json"

for i in {1 .. 20}

do

az deployment group create \

-- resource-group $RESOURCE GROUP
-- template-file $TEMPLATE FILE

-- parameters @$ {PARAMETERS FILE}

done

